AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:16236996") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22073174") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:16236996") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A16236996+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Approximately 88% of cohort members donated a urine sample (n = 65,755) and a blood (n = 56,832) or exfoliated buccal cell (n = 8,934) sample.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000553> "A population-based prospective cohort of 74,942 Chinese women aged 40–70 recruited in 1996–2000 from selected urban communities of Shanghai, most of whom gave blood, urine or buccal-cell samples, followed by biennial in-person recontact and linkage to cancer and vital statistics registries, to study environmental exposures, biomarkers and the risk of cancer and other chronic diseases.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:16236996") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22073174") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:16236996") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A16236996+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Currently, this cohort of women is being followed via biennial in-person recontact and periodic linkage to cancer and vital statistics registries.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000553> "A population-based prospective cohort of 74,942 Chinese women aged 40–70 recruited in 1996–2000 from selected urban communities of Shanghai, most of whom gave blood, urine or buccal-cell samples, followed by biennial in-person recontact and linkage to cancer and vital statistics registries, to study environmental exposures, biomarkers and the risk of cancer and other chronic diseases.")
