# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001182> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001182>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:16822851") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19107115") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fmp.2012.21/MediaObjects/41380_2013_BFmp201221_MOESM194_ESM.doc") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:16822851") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A16822851+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "We investigated 29 single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) within the P2RX7 gene and adjacent genes in a sample of 1000 German Caucasian patients suffering from recurrent major depressive disorder (MDD).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001182> "A case-control sample for recurrent major depressive disorder, collected with GlaxoSmithKline, of German patients with recurrent moderate or severe major depression recruited at the Max Planck Institute of Psychiatry in Munich and two satellite hospitals in the Munich area (BKH Augsburg and Klinikum Ingolstadt), and age- and sex-matched controls from a Munich community sample screened free of lifetime mood and anxiety disorders, genotyped genome-wide for studies of the genetics of depression.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:16822851") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19107115") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fmp.2012.21/MediaObjects/41380_2013_BFmp201221_MOESM194_ESM.doc") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3112053/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The Southern German Recurrent Depression Replication sample included 920 patients (302 males, 618 females) suffering from recurrent major depression (Lucae et al., 2006; Muglia et al., 2008) as well as 1024 controls matched to the patient sample for age, gender and ethnicity.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001182> "A case-control sample for recurrent major depressive disorder, collected with GlaxoSmithKline, of German patients with recurrent moderate or severe major depression recruited at the Max Planck Institute of Psychiatry in Munich and two satellite hospitals in the Munich area (BKH Augsburg and Klinikum Ingolstadt), and age- and sex-matched controls from a Munich community sample screened free of lifetime mood and anxiety disorders, genotyped genome-wide for studies of the genetics of depression.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:16822851") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19107115") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fmp.2012.21/MediaObjects/41380_2013_BFmp201221_MOESM194_ESM.doc") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4309466/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "GSK-Munich and BACCs samples were genotyped on Illumina HumanHap550-Quad+ BeadChip microarrays (Illumina, Inc., San Diego, CA).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001182> "A case-control sample for recurrent major depressive disorder, collected with GlaxoSmithKline, of German patients with recurrent moderate or severe major depression recruited at the Max Planck Institute of Psychiatry in Munich and two satellite hospitals in the Munich area (BKH Augsburg and Klinikum Ingolstadt), and age- and sex-matched controls from a Munich community sample screened free of lifetime mood and anxiety disorders, genotyped genome-wide for studies of the genetics of depression.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:16822851") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19107115") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fmp.2012.21/MediaObjects/41380_2013_BFmp201221_MOESM194_ESM.doc") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4407390/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Controls were selected randomly from a Munich-based community and were screened for the presence of anxiety or mood disorders using the Composite International Diagnostic Screener (German version) [19].") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001182> "A case-control sample for recurrent major depressive disorder, collected with GlaxoSmithKline, of German patients with recurrent moderate or severe major depression recruited at the Max Planck Institute of Psychiatry in Munich and two satellite hospitals in the Munich area (BKH Augsburg and Klinikum Ingolstadt), and age- and sex-matched controls from a Munich community sample screened free of lifetime mood and anxiety disorders, genotyped genome-wide for studies of the genetics of depression.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:16822851") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19107115") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fmp.2012.21/MediaObjects/41380_2013_BFmp201221_MOESM194_ESM.doc") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4407390/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Overall, 1,679 participants (822 cases and 857 controls) were recruited at the Max-Planck Institute of Psychiatry in Munich, Germany, and at two psychiatric hospitals in the Munich area (BKH Augsburg and Klinikum Ingolstadt).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001182> "A case-control sample for recurrent major depressive disorder, collected with GlaxoSmithKline, of German patients with recurrent moderate or severe major depression recruited at the Max Planck Institute of Psychiatry in Munich and two satellite hospitals in the Munich area (BKH Augsburg and Klinikum Ingolstadt), and age- and sex-matched controls from a Munich community sample screened free of lifetime mood and anxiety disorders, genotyped genome-wide for studies of the genetics of depression.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:16822851") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19107115") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fmp.2012.21/MediaObjects/41380_2013_BFmp201221_MOESM194_ESM.doc") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4407390/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Patients had to fulfil the diagnosis of recurrent major depressive disorder of moderate or severe intensity using the SCAN interview.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001182> "A case-control sample for recurrent major depressive disorder, collected with GlaxoSmithKline, of German patients with recurrent moderate or severe major depression recruited at the Max Planck Institute of Psychiatry in Munich and two satellite hospitals in the Munich area (BKH Augsburg and Klinikum Ingolstadt), and age- and sex-matched controls from a Munich community sample screened free of lifetime mood and anxiety disorders, genotyped genome-wide for studies of the genetics of depression.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001183> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001183>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23461817") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:24342383") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:8956679") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:23461817") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC3609892/unicode") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Parents (usually mothers) of the first stage random population sample completed a questionnaire about their child’s behavioral problems.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001183> "A longitudinal, population-based study of children from 11 predominantly rural counties of western North Carolina, United States, recruited in 1993 as three cohorts aged 9, 11 and 13 through a two-phase design that screened a random sample of children for behavioural problems and oversampled those with high scores, together with all age-eligible American Indian children of the area. The participants were interviewed annually to age 16 and then repeatedly into adulthood, to study the development of psychiatric and substance use disorders and the need for and use of mental health services.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23461817") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:24342383") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:8956679") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:23461817") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC3609892/unicode") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Three cohorts of boys and girls, aged 9,11, and 13 at intake in 1993, were selected from a rural population of some 20,000 children using a household equal probability design.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001183> "A longitudinal, population-based study of children from 11 predominantly rural counties of western North Carolina, United States, recruited in 1993 as three cohorts aged 9, 11 and 13 through a two-phase design that screened a random sample of children for behavioural problems and oversampled those with high scores, together with all age-eligible American Indian children of the area. The participants were interviewed annually to age 16 and then repeatedly into adulthood, to study the development of psychiatric and substance use disorders and the need for and use of mental health services.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23461817") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:24342383") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:8956679") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:24342383") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3939681/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "A screening-stratified sampling design achieves both aims by oversampling children scoring high on a screener, and then using sampling weights related to probability of selection to produce unbiased prevalence estimates5.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001183> "A longitudinal, population-based study of children from 11 predominantly rural counties of western North Carolina, United States, recruited in 1993 as three cohorts aged 9, 11 and 13 through a two-phase design that screened a random sample of children for behavioural problems and oversampled those with high scores, together with all age-eligible American Indian children of the area. The participants were interviewed annually to age 16 and then repeatedly into adulthood, to study the development of psychiatric and substance use disorders and the need for and use of mental health services.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23461817") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:24342383") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:8956679") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:24342383") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3939681/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "This paper will review one such study that was begun in the early 1990s to study the development of childhood psychopathology in 11 rural counties in western North Carolina3.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001183> "A longitudinal, population-based study of children from 11 predominantly rural counties of western North Carolina, United States, recruited in 1993 as three cohorts aged 9, 11 and 13 through a two-phase design that screened a random sample of children for behavioural problems and oversampled those with high scores, together with all age-eligible American Indian children of the area. The participants were interviewed annually to age 16 and then repeatedly into adulthood, to study the development of psychiatric and substance use disorders and the need for and use of mental health services.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23461817") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:24342383") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:8956679") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:24342383") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3939681/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "This strategy of annual assessments was continued through age 16 with a few exceptions due to financial restraints.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001183> "A longitudinal, population-based study of children from 11 predominantly rural counties of western North Carolina, United States, recruited in 1993 as three cohorts aged 9, 11 and 13 through a two-phase design that screened a random sample of children for behavioural problems and oversampled those with high scores, together with all age-eligible American Indian children of the area. The participants were interviewed annually to age 16 and then repeatedly into adulthood, to study the development of psychiatric and substance use disorders and the need for and use of mental health services.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23461817") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:24342383") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:8956679") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:8956679") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A8956679+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "In a parallel study, all American Indian children aged 9, 11, and 13 years were recruited (N = 323 at wave 1).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001183> "A longitudinal, population-based study of children from 11 predominantly rural counties of western North Carolina, United States, recruited in 1993 as three cohorts aged 9, 11 and 13 through a two-phase design that screened a random sample of children for behavioural problems and oversampled those with high scores, together with all age-eligible American Indian children of the area. The participants were interviewed annually to age 16 and then repeatedly into adulthood, to study the development of psychiatric and substance use disorders and the need for and use of mental health services.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001184> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001184>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:35693796") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT02391675") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7562899/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Acute appendicitis was diagnosed at the emergency department of Jessa Hospital, Hasselt, Belgium based on medical history, clinical examination, laboratory results and ultrasound and/or CT scan.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001184> "A prospective cohort of patients aged 5 to 85 who presented with acute appendicitis at the emergency department of Jessa Hospital in Hasselt, Belgium, and had an appendectomy, recruited from June 2012 to October 2016 in a study led by Hasselt University with Jessa Hospital and Radboud University Medical Center. Questionnaires on diet, lifestyle and environment were collected together with blood, appendix tissue and stool samples, to study how environmental factors, the innate immune system and its genetic make-up, and the gut microbiota influence the development and severity of acute appendicitis.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:35693796") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT02391675") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7562899/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Immediately before induction of anesthesia for appendectomy, 2 × 5 ml serum samples were taken (Time point 1).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001184> "A prospective cohort of patients aged 5 to 85 who presented with acute appendicitis at the emergency department of Jessa Hospital in Hasselt, Belgium, and had an appendectomy, recruited from June 2012 to October 2016 in a study led by Hasselt University with Jessa Hospital and Radboud University Medical Center. Questionnaires on diet, lifestyle and environment were collected together with blood, appendix tissue and stool samples, to study how environmental factors, the innate immune system and its genetic make-up, and the gut microbiota influence the development and severity of acute appendicitis.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:35693796") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT02391675") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7562899/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Patients were prospectively recruited in the Hasselt Appendicitis Immunology and Environmental Study (HAPPIEST) cohort between June 2012 and October 2016.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001184> "A prospective cohort of patients aged 5 to 85 who presented with acute appendicitis at the emergency department of Jessa Hospital in Hasselt, Belgium, and had an appendectomy, recruited from June 2012 to October 2016 in a study led by Hasselt University with Jessa Hospital and Radboud University Medical Center. Questionnaires on diet, lifestyle and environment were collected together with blood, appendix tissue and stool samples, to study how environmental factors, the innate immune system and its genetic make-up, and the gut microbiota influence the development and severity of acute appendicitis.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:35693796") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT02391675") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7562899/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Questionnaires covered demographic data such as age, gender, dietary habits, lifestyle characteristics and environmental factors.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001184> "A prospective cohort of patients aged 5 to 85 who presented with acute appendicitis at the emergency department of Jessa Hospital in Hasselt, Belgium, and had an appendectomy, recruited from June 2012 to October 2016 in a study led by Hasselt University with Jessa Hospital and Radboud University Medical Center. Questionnaires on diet, lifestyle and environment were collected together with blood, appendix tissue and stool samples, to study how environmental factors, the innate immune system and its genetic make-up, and the gut microbiota influence the development and severity of acute appendicitis.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:35693796") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT02391675") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7562899/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Therefore, we hypothesized that the risk of appendicitis and the severity of inflammation are dependent on the individual’s innate immune responses towards components of the gut microbiota.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001184> "A prospective cohort of patients aged 5 to 85 who presented with acute appendicitis at the emergency department of Jessa Hospital in Hasselt, Belgium, and had an appendectomy, recruited from June 2012 to October 2016 in a study led by Hasselt University with Jessa Hospital and Radboud University Medical Center. Questionnaires on diet, lifestyle and environment were collected together with blood, appendix tissue and stool samples, to study how environmental factors, the innate immune system and its genetic make-up, and the gut microbiota influence the development and severity of acute appendicitis.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:33060696") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:35693796") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT02391675") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:37128345") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10147974/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Patients with acute appendicitis between the ages of 5 and 85 were considered eligible.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001184> "A prospective cohort of patients aged 5 to 85 who presented with acute appendicitis at the emergency department of Jessa Hospital in Hasselt, Belgium, and had an appendectomy, recruited from June 2012 to October 2016 in a study led by Hasselt University with Jessa Hospital and Radboud University Medical Center. Questionnaires on diet, lifestyle and environment were collected together with blood, appendix tissue and stool samples, to study how environmental factors, the innate immune system and its genetic make-up, and the gut microbiota influence the development and severity of acute appendicitis.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001185> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001185>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:32139696") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:32139696") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41467-020-15046-w/MediaObjects/41467_2020_15046_MOESM1_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Controls were 3,186 women who were randomly selected from among 98 women with no previous cancer diagnosis at enrollment in the Namwon Study and the 99 Dong-gu study, ongoing community-based cohort studies in South Korea.8") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001185> "The breast cancer arm of the Hwasun Cancer Epidemiology Study, a hospital-based case-control study at Chonnam National University Hwasun Hospital, a cancer hospital in Jeollanam-do province, South Korea, that aims to identify factors in cancer development and clinical progression in a Korean population. Its cases are women newly diagnosed with breast cancer at the hospital from April 2004 to February 2013, excluding secondary or recurrent tumours, and its controls are women with no previous cancer drawn at random from the Namwon Study and the Dong-gu Study, community-based cohorts in South Korea.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:32139696") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:32139696") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41467-020-15046-w/MediaObjects/41467_2020_15046_MOESM1_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Included in this project were 3,387 female breast 94 cancer cases, who were newly diagnosed between April 2004 and February 2013 at 95 Chonnam National University Hwasun Hospital, a cancer specified hospital in 96 Jeollanam-do province, South Korea.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001185> "The breast cancer arm of the Hwasun Cancer Epidemiology Study, a hospital-based case-control study at Chonnam National University Hwasun Hospital, a cancer hospital in Jeollanam-do province, South Korea, that aims to identify factors in cancer development and clinical progression in a Korean population. Its cases are women newly diagnosed with breast cancer at the hospital from April 2004 to February 2013, excluding secondary or recurrent tumours, and its controls are women with no previous cancer drawn at random from the Namwon Study and the Dong-gu Study, community-based cohorts in South Korea.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:32139696") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:32139696") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41467-020-15046-w/MediaObjects/41467_2020_15046_MOESM1_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Patients with secondary or recurrent tumor were 97 excluded.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001185> "The breast cancer arm of the Hwasun Cancer Epidemiology Study, a hospital-based case-control study at Chonnam National University Hwasun Hospital, a cancer hospital in Jeollanam-do province, South Korea, that aims to identify factors in cancer development and clinical progression in a Korean population. Its cases are women newly diagnosed with breast cancer at the hospital from April 2004 to February 2013, excluding secondary or recurrent tumours, and its controls are women with no previous cancer drawn at random from the Namwon Study and the Dong-gu Study, community-based cohorts in South Korea.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:32139696") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:32139696") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41467-020-15046-w/MediaObjects/41467_2020_15046_MOESM1_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The Hwasun Cancer Epidemiology Study (HCES-Br) is a hospital-based case-control 92 study whose goal is to identify factors of the cancer development and clinical 93 progression in a Korean population.6,7") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001185> "The breast cancer arm of the Hwasun Cancer Epidemiology Study, a hospital-based case-control study at Chonnam National University Hwasun Hospital, a cancer hospital in Jeollanam-do province, South Korea, that aims to identify factors in cancer development and clinical progression in a Korean population. Its cases are women newly diagnosed with breast cancer at the hospital from April 2004 to February 2013, excluding secondary or recurrent tumours, and its controls are women with no previous cancer drawn at random from the Namwon Study and the Dong-gu Study, community-based cohorts in South Korea.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001186> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001186>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22587629") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23022702") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26372449") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:22587629") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A22587629+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Markers of glucose homeostasis were derived from a 3-point oral glucose tolerance test and included fasting plasma glucose, 2-h plasma glucose, HbA(1c), two measures of insulin sensitivity (the insulin sensitivity index(0,120) and homeostasis model assessment of insulin sensitivity), the homeostasis model assessment of β-cell function and glucose tolerance status.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001186> "A cross-sectional population-based study of the Research Centre for Prevention and Health in Glostrup, Denmark, conducted in 2008–2009 on a random sample of men and women aged 30–60, drawn from the Danish Central Personal Register, living in the western part of the Copenhagen area; pregnant women and people with known diabetes, cardiovascular disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, hypertension or a history of blood clots, or unable to do physical activities such as climbing stairs, were excluded. The examination included questionnaires, clinical and biochemical measurements, an oral glucose tolerance test and two tests estimating maximal oxygen uptake.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22587629") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23022702") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26372449") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:22587629") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A22587629+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The study comprised 771 participants from the Danish, population-based cross-sectional 'Health2008' study.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001186> "A cross-sectional population-based study of the Research Centre for Prevention and Health in Glostrup, Denmark, conducted in 2008–2009 on a random sample of men and women aged 30–60, drawn from the Danish Central Personal Register, living in the western part of the Copenhagen area; pregnant women and people with known diabetes, cardiovascular disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, hypertension or a history of blood clots, or unable to do physical activities such as climbing stairs, were excluded. The examination included questionnaires, clinical and biochemical measurements, an oral glucose tolerance test and two tests estimating maximal oxygen uptake.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22587629") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23022702") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26372449") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:23022702") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A23022702+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "To compare a simple fitness test, the Danish step test, to an indirect maximal test, the watt-max test, for estimation of VO2max.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001186> "A cross-sectional population-based study of the Research Centre for Prevention and Health in Glostrup, Denmark, conducted in 2008–2009 on a random sample of men and women aged 30–60, drawn from the Danish Central Personal Register, living in the western part of the Copenhagen area; pregnant women and people with known diabetes, cardiovascular disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, hypertension or a history of blood clots, or unable to do physical activities such as climbing stairs, were excluded. The examination included questionnaires, clinical and biochemical measurements, an oral glucose tolerance test and two tests estimating maximal oxygen uptake.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22587629") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23022702") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26372449") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26372449") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4570778/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Participants were recruited from the Danish Central Personal Register as random samples of the background population living in the Western part of the Copenhagen Region [22].") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001186> "A cross-sectional population-based study of the Research Centre for Prevention and Health in Glostrup, Denmark, conducted in 2008–2009 on a random sample of men and women aged 30–60, drawn from the Danish Central Personal Register, living in the western part of the Copenhagen area; pregnant women and people with known diabetes, cardiovascular disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, hypertension or a history of blood clots, or unable to do physical activities such as climbing stairs, were excluded. The examination included questionnaires, clinical and biochemical measurements, an oral glucose tolerance test and two tests estimating maximal oxygen uptake.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22587629") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23022702") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26372449") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26372449") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4570778/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Pregnant women, persons with known diabetes, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, cardiovascular disease, hypertension, a history of blood clots, or unable to participate in physical activities such as climbing stairs were excluded from the study.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001186> "A cross-sectional population-based study of the Research Centre for Prevention and Health in Glostrup, Denmark, conducted in 2008–2009 on a random sample of men and women aged 30–60, drawn from the Danish Central Personal Register, living in the western part of the Copenhagen area; pregnant women and people with known diabetes, cardiovascular disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, hypertension or a history of blood clots, or unable to do physical activities such as climbing stairs, were excluded. The examination included questionnaires, clinical and biochemical measurements, an oral glucose tolerance test and two tests estimating maximal oxygen uptake.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22587629") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23022702") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26372449") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26372449") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4570778/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The Health2008 study was conducted between 2008 and 2009 [22,27,28].") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001186> "A cross-sectional population-based study of the Research Centre for Prevention and Health in Glostrup, Denmark, conducted in 2008–2009 on a random sample of men and women aged 30–60, drawn from the Danish Central Personal Register, living in the western part of the Copenhagen area; pregnant women and people with known diabetes, cardiovascular disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, hypertension or a history of blood clots, or unable to do physical activities such as climbing stairs, were excluded. The examination included questionnaires, clinical and biochemical measurements, an oral glucose tolerance test and two tests estimating maximal oxygen uptake.")

