# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001330> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001330>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29410079") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:30267149") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38036604") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39112778") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:42598116") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:30267149") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00406-018-0943-x") Annotation(rdfs:comment "1. n = 700 patients suffering from MDD. 2. n = 300 patients suffering from BD. 3. n = 200 patients suffering from SZ. 4. n = 100 patients suffering from SZA. 5. n = 500 healthy subjects at genetic risk (n = 200), environmental risk (n = 200) or both (n = 100). 6. n = 1000 healthy subjects without any of these risk factors.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001330> "The human cohort of the German Research Foundation (DFG) research unit FOR2107 on the neurobiology of the major psychoses, recruited between 2014 and 2018 at the Universities of Marburg and Münster, Germany, through local psychiatric hospitals and newspaper advertisements: adults aged 18–65 with major depressive disorder, bipolar disorder, schizoaffective disorder or schizophrenia, healthy people at genetic and/or environmental risk, and healthy controls, deeply phenotyped with MRI, clinical course, neuropsychology, personality, risk and protective factors and biomaterials (blood, stool, urine, hair, saliva) at baseline and again at a 2-year follow-up, to identify how genetic and environmental factors shape brain structure and function and the course of these disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29410079") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:30267149") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38036604") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39112778") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:42598116") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:30267149") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00406-018-0943-x") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Participants are followed up after 2 years and twice underwent extensive deep phenotyping (MR imaging, clinical course, neuropsychology, personality, risk/protective factors, biomaterials: blood, stool, urine, hair, saliva).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001330> "The human cohort of the German Research Foundation (DFG) research unit FOR2107 on the neurobiology of the major psychoses, recruited between 2014 and 2018 at the Universities of Marburg and Münster, Germany, through local psychiatric hospitals and newspaper advertisements: adults aged 18–65 with major depressive disorder, bipolar disorder, schizoaffective disorder or schizophrenia, healthy people at genetic and/or environmental risk, and healthy controls, deeply phenotyped with MRI, clinical course, neuropsychology, personality, risk and protective factors and biomaterials (blood, stool, urine, hair, saliva) at baseline and again at a 2-year follow-up, to identify how genetic and environmental factors shape brain structure and function and the course of these disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29410079") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:30267149") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38036604") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39112778") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:42598116") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:30267149") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00406-018-0943-x") Annotation(rdfs:comment "We have characterized n = 2500 individuals across 6 samples with the T1 battery at baseline (first subject in 9/11/2014).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001330> "The human cohort of the German Research Foundation (DFG) research unit FOR2107 on the neurobiology of the major psychoses, recruited between 2014 and 2018 at the Universities of Marburg and Münster, Germany, through local psychiatric hospitals and newspaper advertisements: adults aged 18–65 with major depressive disorder, bipolar disorder, schizoaffective disorder or schizophrenia, healthy people at genetic and/or environmental risk, and healthy controls, deeply phenotyped with MRI, clinical course, neuropsychology, personality, risk and protective factors and biomaterials (blood, stool, urine, hair, saliva) at baseline and again at a 2-year follow-up, to identify how genetic and environmental factors shape brain structure and function and the course of these disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29410079") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:30267149") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38036604") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39112778") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:42598116") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:38036604") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11116107/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The MACS is part of the FOR2107 research consortium that studies risk factors, course of illness, and neurobiological correlates of major psychoses, including MDD [27].") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001330> "The human cohort of the German Research Foundation (DFG) research unit FOR2107 on the neurobiology of the major psychoses, recruited between 2014 and 2018 at the Universities of Marburg and Münster, Germany, through local psychiatric hospitals and newspaper advertisements: adults aged 18–65 with major depressive disorder, bipolar disorder, schizoaffective disorder or schizophrenia, healthy people at genetic and/or environmental risk, and healthy controls, deeply phenotyped with MRI, clinical course, neuropsychology, personality, risk and protective factors and biomaterials (blood, stool, urine, hair, saliva) at baseline and again at a 2-year follow-up, to identify how genetic and environmental factors shape brain structure and function and the course of these disorders.")
