AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19127255") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19240718") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23065074") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:36826816") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:42365137") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:23065074") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3499637/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "In-person interviews were conducted in relation to events that occurred before a woman’s diagnosis/reference date and included demographic and lifestyle factors, family history of cancer and reproductive history.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612> "A population-based case–control study of epithelial ovarian cancer in western Washington State, United States, run from Seattle with funding from the US National Cancer Institute, that enrolled women aged 35–74 diagnosed in 2002–2009 in a 13-county area, identified through the region's SEER cancer registry, together with controls from the same area identified by random-digit dialling, and collected in-person interviews and, from cases, tumour tissue.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19127255") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19240718") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23065074") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:36826816") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:42365137") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:23065074") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3499637/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "They were identified through the Cancer Surveillance System (CSS), a population-based cancer registry participating in the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results Program of the National Cancer Institute [17]; of the potentially eligible women, 61 cases were excluded due to a language barrier.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612> "A population-based case–control study of epithelial ovarian cancer in western Washington State, United States, run from Seattle with funding from the US National Cancer Institute, that enrolled women aged 35–74 diagnosed in 2002–2009 in a 13-county area, identified through the region's SEER cancer registry, together with controls from the same area identified by random-digit dialling, and collected in-person interviews and, from cases, tumour tissue.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19127255") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19240718") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23065074") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:36826816") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:42365137") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:36826816") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC9958519/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The Diseases of the Ovary and Their Evaluation Study was funded by grants R01 CA112523 and R01 CA87538 from the NCI.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612> "A population-based case–control study of epithelial ovarian cancer in western Washington State, United States, run from Seattle with funding from the US National Cancer Institute, that enrolled women aged 35–74 diagnosed in 2002–2009 in a 13-county area, identified through the region's SEER cancer registry, together with controls from the same area identified by random-digit dialling, and collected in-person interviews and, from cases, tumour tissue.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19127255") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19240718") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23065074") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:36826816") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:42365137") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:42365137") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13534713/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "DOVE is a population-based ovarian cancer case-control study that enrolled 1502 ovarian cancer cases ages 35–74 years from 13 counties in Western Washington State between 2002 and 2009 [19].") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612> "A population-based case–control study of epithelial ovarian cancer in western Washington State, United States, run from Seattle with funding from the US National Cancer Institute, that enrolled women aged 35–74 diagnosed in 2002–2009 in a 13-county area, identified through the region's SEER cancer registry, together with controls from the same area identified by random-digit dialling, and collected in-person interviews and, from cases, tumour tissue.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19127255") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19240718") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23065074") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:36826816") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:42365137") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:42365137") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13534713/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "There were 149 cases from AACES who provided tumor samples for tissue microarrays (TMAs), 1080 from DOVE, 53 from HOPE, 398 from NECC, and 530 from NHS/NHSII.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612> "A population-based case–control study of epithelial ovarian cancer in western Washington State, United States, run from Seattle with funding from the US National Cancer Institute, that enrolled women aged 35–74 diagnosed in 2002–2009 in a 13-county area, identified through the region's SEER cancer registry, together with controls from the same area identified by random-digit dialling, and collected in-person interviews and, from cases, tumour tissue.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001613> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001613>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21775352") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26604824") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:9062767") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21775352") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A21775352+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The PCRR is a valuable tool in national health planning and in epidemiological research.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001613> "A nationwide Danish register of patients treated in psychiatric departments, with records collected systematically from 1938 and computerised from 1969, covering all admissions to psychiatric hospitals and psychiatric wards of general hospitals in Denmark, the Faroe Islands and Greenland and, since 1995, all psychiatric outpatient contacts, with dates of treatment, diagnoses, referral, place and mode of admission and municipality of residence. Kept within a psychiatric epidemiology research unit and fed since 1995 through the Danish National Patient Register, it is used for national health planning and psychiatric epidemiological research.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21775352") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26604824") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:9062767") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21775352") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A21775352+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The register contains dates of onset and end of any treatment; all diagnoses; type of referral; place of treatment; municipality of residence; mode of admission.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001613> "A nationwide Danish register of patients treated in psychiatric departments, with records collected systematically from 1938 and computerised from 1969, covering all admissions to psychiatric hospitals and psychiatric wards of general hospitals in Denmark, the Faroe Islands and Greenland and, since 1995, all psychiatric outpatient contacts, with dates of treatment, diagnoses, referral, place and mode of admission and municipality of residence. Kept within a psychiatric epidemiology research unit and fed since 1995 through the Danish National Patient Register, it is used for national health planning and psychiatric epidemiological research.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21775352") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26604824") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:9062767") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26604824") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4655913/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The registry also collects data for other health registries, including the Danish Psychiatric Central Research Register since 1995,31 the Register of Legally Induced Abortions since 1995,32 the Medical Birth Registry since 1997,33 and the Danish Cancer Registry since 2004.34") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001613> "A nationwide Danish register of patients treated in psychiatric departments, with records collected systematically from 1938 and computerised from 1969, covering all admissions to psychiatric hospitals and psychiatric wards of general hospitals in Denmark, the Faroe Islands and Greenland and, since 1995, all psychiatric outpatient contacts, with dates of treatment, diagnoses, referral, place and mode of admission and municipality of residence. Kept within a psychiatric epidemiology research unit and fed since 1995 through the Danish National Patient Register, it is used for national health planning and psychiatric epidemiological research.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21775352") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26604824") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:9062767") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:9062767") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A9062767+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "As of 1969 data on psychiatric admissions has been computerized and includes all admissions to psychiatric hospitals and psychiatric wards in general hospitals in Denmark, the Faroe Islands, and Greenland.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001613> "A nationwide Danish register of patients treated in psychiatric departments, with records collected systematically from 1938 and computerised from 1969, covering all admissions to psychiatric hospitals and psychiatric wards of general hospitals in Denmark, the Faroe Islands and Greenland and, since 1995, all psychiatric outpatient contacts, with dates of treatment, diagnoses, referral, place and mode of admission and municipality of residence. Kept within a psychiatric epidemiology research unit and fed since 1995 through the Danish National Patient Register, it is used for national health planning and psychiatric epidemiological research.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21775352") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26604824") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:9062767") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:9062767") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A9062767+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Since January 1, 1995 information about all psychiatric outpatient contacts has been included.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001613> "A nationwide Danish register of patients treated in psychiatric departments, with records collected systematically from 1938 and computerised from 1969, covering all admissions to psychiatric hospitals and psychiatric wards of general hospitals in Denmark, the Faroe Islands and Greenland and, since 1995, all psychiatric outpatient contacts, with dates of treatment, diagnoses, referral, place and mode of admission and municipality of residence. Kept within a psychiatric epidemiology research unit and fed since 1995 through the Danish National Patient Register, it is used for national health planning and psychiatric epidemiological research.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21775352") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26604824") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:9062767") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:9062767") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A9062767+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The Danish Psychiatric Central Register contains information dating from the 19th century; data were collected systematically from 1938.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001613> "A nationwide Danish register of patients treated in psychiatric departments, with records collected systematically from 1938 and computerised from 1969, covering all admissions to psychiatric hospitals and psychiatric wards of general hospitals in Denmark, the Faroe Islands and Greenland and, since 1995, all psychiatric outpatient contacts, with dates of treatment, diagnoses, referral, place and mode of admission and municipality of residence. Kept within a psychiatric epidemiology research unit and fed since 1995 through the Danish National Patient Register, it is used for national health planning and psychiatric epidemiological research.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001614> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001614>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22363532") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27418945") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29696206") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:22363532") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3281903/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "All consecutive patients ≥18 years of age with the suspicion of a pelvic mass were informed both in writing and verbally and were invited after written consent to participate in the study.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001614> "A prospective Danish study that from September 2004 enrolled consecutive women aged 18 or older admitted for surgery because of a suspected pelvic mass to the Gynaecologic Clinic of Copenhagen University Hospital, Rigshospitalet, collecting their blood and tumour tissue with clinical data to identify factors that distinguish benign from malignant pelvic masses and predict the prognosis of ovarian cancer.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22363532") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27418945") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29696206") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:22363532") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3281903/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "From September 2004 559 women admitted to the Gynecologic Clinic, Rigshospitalet, Denmark for surgery because of a pelvic mass, were enrolled.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001614> "A prospective Danish study that from September 2004 enrolled consecutive women aged 18 or older admitted for surgery because of a suspected pelvic mass to the Gynaecologic Clinic of Copenhagen University Hospital, Rigshospitalet, collecting their blood and tumour tissue with clinical data to identify factors that distinguish benign from malignant pelvic masses and predict the prognosis of ovarian cancer.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22363532") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27418945") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29696206") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:22363532") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3281903/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The Pelvic Mass study is a Danish prospective study of ovarian cancer, covering biochemistry and molecular biology with the purpose of identifying prognostic factors as well as factors that differentiate benign and malignant conditions.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001614> "A prospective Danish study that from September 2004 enrolled consecutive women aged 18 or older admitted for surgery because of a suspected pelvic mass to the Gynaecologic Clinic of Copenhagen University Hospital, Rigshospitalet, collecting their blood and tumour tissue with clinical data to identify factors that distinguish benign from malignant pelvic masses and predict the prognosis of ovarian cancer.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22363532") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27418945") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29696206") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:29696206") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5898550/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "All blood and tissue samples (fresh frozen, RNA-later treated, formalin fixed and paraffin embedded tissues (FFPE) and O.C.T. compounds) are registered and stored in Danish Cancer Biobank (www.cancerbiobank.dk), and handled according to their guidelines.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001614> "A prospective Danish study that from September 2004 enrolled consecutive women aged 18 or older admitted for surgery because of a suspected pelvic mass to the Gynaecologic Clinic of Copenhagen University Hospital, Rigshospitalet, collecting their blood and tumour tissue with clinical data to identify factors that distinguish benign from malignant pelvic masses and predict the prognosis of ovarian cancer.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22363532") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27418945") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29696206") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:29696206") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5898550/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "All patient tissue is examined by specialized pathologists and the patients are registered in the Danish Gynecologic Cancer Database (DGCD, http://www.dgcg.dk/), where information about clinical data, treatment and survival of the patient is updated regularly.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001614> "A prospective Danish study that from September 2004 enrolled consecutive women aged 18 or older admitted for surgery because of a suspected pelvic mass to the Gynaecologic Clinic of Copenhagen University Hospital, Rigshospitalet, collecting their blood and tumour tissue with clinical data to identify factors that distinguish benign from malignant pelvic masses and predict the prognosis of ovarian cancer.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:22363532") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27418945") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29696206") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:29696206") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5898550/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Patients are included if they are 18 years or older and admitted to surgery of a potentially malignant pelvic mass at the Gynecologic clinic at Copenhagen University Hospital, Rigshospitalet (Fig. 1).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001614> "A prospective Danish study that from September 2004 enrolled consecutive women aged 18 or older admitted for surgery because of a suspected pelvic mass to the Gynaecologic Clinic of Copenhagen University Hospital, Rigshospitalet, collecting their blood and tumour tissue with clinical data to identify factors that distinguish benign from malignant pelvic masses and predict the prognosis of ovarian cancer.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001615> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001615>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20437058") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27142380") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://doi.org/10.1038/ng2089") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Cancer Care Ontario, as the host organization to the ARCTIC Genome Project, acknowledges that this Project was funded by Genome Canada through the Ontario Genomics Institute, by Génome Québec, the Ministère du Dévelopement Économique et Régional et de la Recherche du Québec and the Ontario Institute for Cancer Research") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001615> "A colorectal cancer genomics project in Ontario, Canada (the ARCTIC Genome Project), hosted by Cancer Care Ontario and funded by Genome Canada through the Ontario Genomics Institute and other agencies, whose case-control sample consists of participants of the Ontario Familial Colorectal Cancer Registry: people aged 20–74 with colorectal cancer diagnosed in 1997–2000 and identified through the population-based Ontario Cancer Registry, and population controls randomly selected and frequency-matched to them by sex and five-year age group. They were genotyped genome-wide to find common variants associated with colorectal cancer, and the sample has since been used for risk-prediction and DNA methylation studies.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20437058") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27142380") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://doi.org/10.1038/ng2089") Annotation(rdfs:comment "In stage 1, we genotyped 99,632 SNPs in 1,257 affected individuals and 1,336 controls from Ontario.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001615> "A colorectal cancer genomics project in Ontario, Canada (the ARCTIC Genome Project), hosted by Cancer Care Ontario and funded by Genome Canada through the Ontario Genomics Institute and other agencies, whose case-control sample consists of participants of the Ontario Familial Colorectal Cancer Registry: people aged 20–74 with colorectal cancer diagnosed in 1997–2000 and identified through the population-based Ontario Cancer Registry, and population controls randomly selected and frequency-matched to them by sex and five-year age group. They were genotyped genome-wide to find common variants associated with colorectal cancer, and the sample has since been used for risk-prediction and DNA methylation studies.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20437058") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27142380") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng2089/MediaObjects/41588_2007_BFng2089_MOESM20_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Population-based controls were randomly selected and frequency-matched, within sex and five-year age groups, to the colorectal cancer cases.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001615> "A colorectal cancer genomics project in Ontario, Canada (the ARCTIC Genome Project), hosted by Cancer Care Ontario and funded by Genome Canada through the Ontario Genomics Institute and other agencies, whose case-control sample consists of participants of the Ontario Familial Colorectal Cancer Registry: people aged 20–74 with colorectal cancer diagnosed in 1997–2000 and identified through the population-based Ontario Cancer Registry, and population controls randomly selected and frequency-matched to them by sex and five-year age group. They were genotyped genome-wide to find common variants associated with colorectal cancer, and the sample has since been used for risk-prediction and DNA methylation studies.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20437058") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27142380") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng2089/MediaObjects/41588_2007_BFng2089_MOESM20_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The population-based Ontario Cancer Registry (OCR) was used to identify and recruit colorectal cancer cases, aged 20-74 and diagnosed between July 1, 1997 and June 30, 2000.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001615> "A colorectal cancer genomics project in Ontario, Canada (the ARCTIC Genome Project), hosted by Cancer Care Ontario and funded by Genome Canada through the Ontario Genomics Institute and other agencies, whose case-control sample consists of participants of the Ontario Familial Colorectal Cancer Registry: people aged 20–74 with colorectal cancer diagnosed in 1997–2000 and identified through the population-based Ontario Cancer Registry, and population controls randomly selected and frequency-matched to them by sex and five-year age group. They were genotyped genome-wide to find common variants associated with colorectal cancer, and the sample has since been used for risk-prediction and DNA methylation studies.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20437058") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27142380") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:20437058") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2885303/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Briefly, the ARCTIC study involved 1,257 CRC cases and 1,336 matched community controls from the Ontario Familial Colorectal Cancer Registry.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001615> "A colorectal cancer genomics project in Ontario, Canada (the ARCTIC Genome Project), hosted by Cancer Care Ontario and funded by Genome Canada through the Ontario Genomics Institute and other agencies, whose case-control sample consists of participants of the Ontario Familial Colorectal Cancer Registry: people aged 20–74 with colorectal cancer diagnosed in 1997–2000 and identified through the population-based Ontario Cancer Registry, and population controls randomly selected and frequency-matched to them by sex and five-year age group. They were genotyped genome-wide to find common variants associated with colorectal cancer, and the sample has since been used for risk-prediction and DNA methylation studies.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17618283") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20437058") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27142380") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:27142380") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4855979/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "We tested the performance of these eight adjustment methods on 450K measurements from the Assessment of Risk in Colorectal Tumors in Canada (ARCTIC) study [32], and the methylation data are deposited in dbGAP under accession number [phs000779.v1.p1].") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001615> "A colorectal cancer genomics project in Ontario, Canada (the ARCTIC Genome Project), hosted by Cancer Care Ontario and funded by Genome Canada through the Ontario Genomics Institute and other agencies, whose case-control sample consists of participants of the Ontario Familial Colorectal Cancer Registry: people aged 20–74 with colorectal cancer diagnosed in 1997–2000 and identified through the population-based Ontario Cancer Registry, and population controls randomly selected and frequency-matched to them by sex and five-year age group. They were genotyped genome-wide to find common variants associated with colorectal cancer, and the sample has since been used for risk-prediction and DNA methylation studies.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001616> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001616>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:31303884") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37454130") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3169352/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Currently, seven cancer institutions are participating in the BCCR that contains data on almost 900 subjects (BC patients and survivors, as well as individuals at high risk of getting BC).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001616> "A web-based multicentre breast cancer registry developed at the University of Nebraska Medical Center's Eppley Cancer Center in Omaha, Nebraska, United States, and maintained by the Fred & Pamela Buffett Cancer Center as part of its integrated Cancer Repository for Cancer Research (iCaRe2), into which participating cancer centres in the US Midwest enrol breast cancer patients, breast cancer survivors and women at increased risk of breast cancer. Under common protocols it collects questionnaire data on demographic, medical, family, lifestyle, dietary and environmental factors, clinical and follow-up data and biospecimens for research on breast cancer diagnosis, prevention, treatment and survivorship.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:31303884") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37454130") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3169352/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The BCCR collects a variety of information, including demographic, medical and family details, dietary and environmental exposure history, as well as biospecimen samples from breast cancer patients, women at increased risk for breast cancer (those with one or more first-degree relatives or at least two second-degree relatives with a history of cancer), and BC survivors.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001616> "A web-based multicentre breast cancer registry developed at the University of Nebraska Medical Center's Eppley Cancer Center in Omaha, Nebraska, United States, and maintained by the Fred & Pamela Buffett Cancer Center as part of its integrated Cancer Repository for Cancer Research (iCaRe2), into which participating cancer centres in the US Midwest enrol breast cancer patients, breast cancer survivors and women at increased risk of breast cancer. Under common protocols it collects questionnaire data on demographic, medical, family, lifestyle, dietary and environmental factors, clinical and follow-up data and biospecimens for research on breast cancer diagnosis, prevention, treatment and survivorship.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:31303884") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37454130") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3169352/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The BCCR questionnaires have been designed and developed to collect comprehensive data related to the diagnosis, treatment and follow-up of BC patients, as well as information pertaining to demographics and survivorship.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001616> "A web-based multicentre breast cancer registry developed at the University of Nebraska Medical Center's Eppley Cancer Center in Omaha, Nebraska, United States, and maintained by the Fred & Pamela Buffett Cancer Center as part of its integrated Cancer Repository for Cancer Research (iCaRe2), into which participating cancer centres in the US Midwest enrol breast cancer patients, breast cancer survivors and women at increased risk of breast cancer. Under common protocols it collects questionnaire data on demographic, medical, family, lifestyle, dietary and environmental factors, clinical and follow-up data and biospecimens for research on breast cancer diagnosis, prevention, treatment and survivorship.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:31303884") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37454130") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3169352/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The Breast Cancer Collaborative Registry (BCCR) is a multicenter web-based system that efficiently collects and manages a variety of data on breast cancer (BC) patients and BC survivors.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001616> "A web-based multicentre breast cancer registry developed at the University of Nebraska Medical Center's Eppley Cancer Center in Omaha, Nebraska, United States, and maintained by the Fred & Pamela Buffett Cancer Center as part of its integrated Cancer Repository for Cancer Research (iCaRe2), into which participating cancer centres in the US Midwest enrol breast cancer patients, breast cancer survivors and women at increased risk of breast cancer. Under common protocols it collects questionnaire data on demographic, medical, family, lifestyle, dietary and environmental factors, clinical and follow-up data and biospecimens for research on breast cancer diagnosis, prevention, treatment and survivorship.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:31303884") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37454130") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:31303884") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC6611482/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The BCCR, which is a part of the integrated Cancer Repository for Cancer Research (iCaRe2), was developed in collaboration with breast cancer experts and research questions were standardized to satisfy the needs of all the centers [21].") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001616> "A web-based multicentre breast cancer registry developed at the University of Nebraska Medical Center's Eppley Cancer Center in Omaha, Nebraska, United States, and maintained by the Fred & Pamela Buffett Cancer Center as part of its integrated Cancer Repository for Cancer Research (iCaRe2), into which participating cancer centres in the US Midwest enrol breast cancer patients, breast cancer survivors and women at increased risk of breast cancer. Under common protocols it collects questionnaire data on demographic, medical, family, lifestyle, dietary and environmental factors, clinical and follow-up data and biospecimens for research on breast cancer diagnosis, prevention, treatment and survivorship.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21918596") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:31303884") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37454130") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:37454130") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10349437/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The Fred and Pamela Buffett Cancer Center (FPBCC) and its affiliated hospital network have been maintaining a unique resource, the Breast Cancer Collaborative Registry (BCCR) [22], which is a part of the integrated Cancer Repository for Cancer Research (iCaRe2, https://icare2.unmc.edu).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001616> "A web-based multicentre breast cancer registry developed at the University of Nebraska Medical Center's Eppley Cancer Center in Omaha, Nebraska, United States, and maintained by the Fred & Pamela Buffett Cancer Center as part of its integrated Cancer Repository for Cancer Research (iCaRe2), into which participating cancer centres in the US Midwest enrol breast cancer patients, breast cancer survivors and women at increased risk of breast cancer. Under common protocols it collects questionnaire data on demographic, medical, family, lifestyle, dietary and environmental factors, clinical and follow-up data and biospecimens for research on breast cancer diagnosis, prevention, treatment and survivorship.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001617> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001617>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:34002096") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38355785") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:38355785") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11408248/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Cases from the UK were obtained from the Bipolar Disorder Research Network (BDRN) study, an ongoing program of research into the genetic and environmental causes of BD and related mood disorders.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001617> "A cohort of adults of European ancestry with bipolar disorder (bipolar I or II disorder, schizoaffective disorder of bipolar type or bipolar disorder not otherwise specified) recruited from across the United Kingdom by the Bipolar Disorder Research Network, an ongoing research programme run from Cardiff and Worcester, systematically through NHS community mental health teams and lithium clinics and non-systematically through media advertising and patient charities such as Bipolar UK. Participants are interviewed by trained psychologists and psychiatrists with the Schedules for Clinical Assessment in Neuropsychiatry and their case notes reviewed, to study the genetic and environmental causes of bipolar and related mood disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:34002096") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38355785") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:38355785") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11408248/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "In the UK cohort, subphenotypes were derived from clinical assessments done retrospectively by SCAN interview (assessed when the participants’ moods were stable) and available case-notes review.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001617> "A cohort of adults of European ancestry with bipolar disorder (bipolar I or II disorder, schizoaffective disorder of bipolar type or bipolar disorder not otherwise specified) recruited from across the United Kingdom by the Bipolar Disorder Research Network, an ongoing research programme run from Cardiff and Worcester, systematically through NHS community mental health teams and lithium clinics and non-systematically through media advertising and patient charities such as Bipolar UK. Participants are interviewed by trained psychologists and psychiatrists with the Schedules for Clinical Assessment in Neuropsychiatry and their case notes reviewed, to study the genetic and environmental causes of bipolar and related mood disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:34002096") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38355785") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12092478/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "(v) of European ancestry due to the focus of the research programme on genetic risk factors.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001617> "A cohort of adults of European ancestry with bipolar disorder (bipolar I or II disorder, schizoaffective disorder of bipolar type or bipolar disorder not otherwise specified) recruited from across the United Kingdom by the Bipolar Disorder Research Network, an ongoing research programme run from Cardiff and Worcester, systematically through NHS community mental health teams and lithium clinics and non-systematically through media advertising and patient charities such as Bipolar UK. Participants are interviewed by trained psychologists and psychiatrists with the Schedules for Clinical Assessment in Neuropsychiatry and their case notes reviewed, to study the genetic and environmental causes of bipolar and related mood disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:34002096") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38355785") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12092478/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Non-systematic recruitment methods included advertising for volunteers using local and national media and through UK-based charities for individuals with BD, for example Bipolar UK.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001617> "A cohort of adults of European ancestry with bipolar disorder (bipolar I or II disorder, schizoaffective disorder of bipolar type or bipolar disorder not otherwise specified) recruited from across the United Kingdom by the Bipolar Disorder Research Network, an ongoing research programme run from Cardiff and Worcester, systematically through NHS community mental health teams and lithium clinics and non-systematically through media advertising and patient charities such as Bipolar UK. Participants are interviewed by trained psychologists and psychiatrists with the Schedules for Clinical Assessment in Neuropsychiatry and their case notes reviewed, to study the genetic and environmental causes of bipolar and related mood disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:34002096") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38355785") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12092478/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Participants were recruited systematically via National Health Service (NHS) psychiatric services (community mental health teams and lithium clinics).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001617> "A cohort of adults of European ancestry with bipolar disorder (bipolar I or II disorder, schizoaffective disorder of bipolar type or bipolar disorder not otherwise specified) recruited from across the United Kingdom by the Bipolar Disorder Research Network, an ongoing research programme run from Cardiff and Worcester, systematically through NHS community mental health teams and lithium clinics and non-systematically through media advertising and patient charities such as Bipolar UK. Participants are interviewed by trained psychologists and psychiatrists with the Schedules for Clinical Assessment in Neuropsychiatry and their case notes reviewed, to study the genetic and environmental causes of bipolar and related mood disorders.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:34002096") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38355785") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:39537796") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12092478/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The research programme inclusion criteria were (i) DSM-IV/DSM-5 diagnosis of bipolar disorder (bipolar disorder type I, bipolar disorder type II, schizoaffective disorder bipolar type or bipolar disorder not otherwise specified) (American Psychiatric Association 2000, 2013), (ii) aged at least 18 years,") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001617> "A cohort of adults of European ancestry with bipolar disorder (bipolar I or II disorder, schizoaffective disorder of bipolar type or bipolar disorder not otherwise specified) recruited from across the United Kingdom by the Bipolar Disorder Research Network, an ongoing research programme run from Cardiff and Worcester, systematically through NHS community mental health teams and lithium clinics and non-systematically through media advertising and patient charities such as Bipolar UK. Participants are interviewed by trained psychologists and psychiatrists with the Schedules for Clinical Assessment in Neuropsychiatry and their case notes reviewed, to study the genetic and environmental causes of bipolar and related mood disorders.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001618> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001618>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21320228") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21320228") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A21320228+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The family high-risk design was used to recruit proband fathers with and without substance use disorder and track their sons longitudinally from late childhood to adulthood.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001618> "A longitudinal family high-risk study of the development of substance use disorder, run in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, United States, by the Center for Education and Drug Abuse Research of the University of Pittsburgh, a Center of Excellence funded by the National Institute on Drug Abuse and completed in 2015, which recruited nuclear families of a father with or without a lifetime substance use disorder (or with another psychiatric disorder), his partner and their biological child aged 10–12 (at first sons only, daughters from its second funding cycle) and followed the index children to age 30; index children and their siblings were genotyped under the Genes, Environment and Development Initiative.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21320228") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The CEDAR genetic study has been augmented by the inclusion of its sample into the whole-genome genotyping effort conducted under the Genes, Environment and Development Initiative (GEDI).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001618> "A longitudinal family high-risk study of the development of substance use disorder, run in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, United States, by the Center for Education and Drug Abuse Research of the University of Pittsburgh, a Center of Excellence funded by the National Institute on Drug Abuse and completed in 2015, which recruited nuclear families of a father with or without a lifetime substance use disorder (or with another psychiatric disorder), his partner and their biological child aged 10–12 (at first sons only, daughters from its second funding cycle) and followed the index children to age 30; index children and their siblings were genotyped under the Genes, Environment and Development Initiative.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21320228") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The CEDAR sample consists of 775 nuclear families comprising an adult male (proband), his spouse/mate, and their biological child who is 10-12 years of age at recruitment.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001618> "A longitudinal family high-risk study of the development of substance use disorder, run in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, United States, by the Center for Education and Drug Abuse Research of the University of Pittsburgh, a Center of Excellence funded by the National Institute on Drug Abuse and completed in 2015, which recruited nuclear families of a father with or without a lifetime substance use disorder (or with another psychiatric disorder), his partner and their biological child aged 10–12 (at first sons only, daughters from its second funding cycle) and followed the index children to age 30; index children and their siblings were genotyped under the Genes, Environment and Development Initiative.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21320228") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The representation of females among ICs is lower than males, due to the later start of recruit-ment (2nd funding cycle; the study originally focused on males).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001618> "A longitudinal family high-risk study of the development of substance use disorder, run in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, United States, by the Center for Education and Drug Abuse Research of the University of Pittsburgh, a Center of Excellence funded by the National Institute on Drug Abuse and completed in 2015, which recruited nuclear families of a father with or without a lifetime substance use disorder (or with another psychiatric disorder), his partner and their biological child aged 10–12 (at first sons only, daughters from its second funding cycle) and followed the index children to age 30; index children and their siblings were genotyped under the Genes, Environment and Development Initiative.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21320228") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(rdfs:comment "This child is designated as index child (IC), and is followed through age 30.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001618> "A longitudinal family high-risk study of the development of substance use disorder, run in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, United States, by the Center for Education and Drug Abuse Research of the University of Pittsburgh, a Center of Excellence funded by the National Institute on Drug Abuse and completed in 2015, which recruited nuclear families of a father with or without a lifetime substance use disorder (or with another psychiatric disorder), his partner and their biological child aged 10–12 (at first sons only, daughters from its second funding cycle) and followed the index children to age 30; index children and their siblings were genotyped under the Genes, Environment and Development Initiative.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21320228") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gap/cgi-bin/study.cgi?study_id=phs001649.v1.p1") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Under its programmatic name, the Center for Education and Drug Abuse research (CEDAR), this Center of Excellence project was completed in 2015.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001618> "A longitudinal family high-risk study of the development of substance use disorder, run in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, United States, by the Center for Education and Drug Abuse Research of the University of Pittsburgh, a Center of Excellence funded by the National Institute on Drug Abuse and completed in 2015, which recruited nuclear families of a father with or without a lifetime substance use disorder (or with another psychiatric disorder), his partner and their biological child aged 10–12 (at first sons only, daughters from its second funding cycle) and followed the index children to age 30; index children and their siblings were genotyped under the Genes, Environment and Development Initiative.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001619> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001619>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20453537") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26806518") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37433967") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:20453537") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A20453537+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Following an initiative by the International Group for the Study of Lithium-Treated Patients (www.IGSLI.org) and the Unit on the Genetic Basis of Mood and Anxiety Disorders at the National Institute of Mental Health,lithium researchers from around the world have formed the Consortium on Lithium Genetics (www.ConLiGen.org)") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001619> "An international consortium of lithium researchers, established in 2008 on the initiative of the International Group for the Study of Lithium-Treated Patients (IGSLI) and the Unit on the Genetic Basis of Mood and Anxiety Disorders of the US National Institute of Mental Health, whose participating sites in Europe, America, Asia and Australia pool genotyped patients with bipolar disorder treated with lithium, their long-term response rated retrospectively on the Alda scale, for genome-wide studies of the genetic basis of lithium response.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20453537") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26806518") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37433967") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26806518") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC4814312/unicode") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Here we report the results of an initial GWAS of lithium response in 2563 BD patients – by far the largest sample to date – using phenotype and genotype data from 22 ConLiGen sites from four continents (Europe, America, Asia and Australia; for details, see Table S1; Supplementary Materials).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001619> "An international consortium of lithium researchers, established in 2008 on the initiative of the International Group for the Study of Lithium-Treated Patients (IGSLI) and the Unit on the Genetic Basis of Mood and Anxiety Disorders of the US National Institute of Mental Health, whose participating sites in Europe, America, Asia and Australia pool genotyped patients with bipolar disorder treated with lithium, their long-term response rated retrospectively on the Alda scale, for genome-wide studies of the genetic basis of lithium response.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20453537") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26806518") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37433967") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26806518") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC4814312/unicode") Annotation(rdfs:comment "In order to overcome the problems inherent in smaller sample sizes, we established the international Consortium on Lithium Genetics (www.ConLiGen.org) in 2008.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001619> "An international consortium of lithium researchers, established in 2008 on the initiative of the International Group for the Study of Lithium-Treated Patients (IGSLI) and the Unit on the Genetic Basis of Mood and Anxiety Disorders of the US National Institute of Mental Health, whose participating sites in Europe, America, Asia and Australia pool genotyped patients with bipolar disorder treated with lithium, their long-term response rated retrospectively on the Alda scale, for genome-wide studies of the genetic basis of lithium response.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20453537") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26806518") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37433967") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26806518") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC4814312/unicode") Annotation(rdfs:comment "We used the “Retrospective Criteria of Long-Term Treatment Response in Research Subjects with Bipolar Disorder” (Alda scale) for the evaluation of long-term treatment response to lithium.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001619> "An international consortium of lithium researchers, established in 2008 on the initiative of the International Group for the Study of Lithium-Treated Patients (IGSLI) and the Unit on the Genetic Basis of Mood and Anxiety Disorders of the US National Institute of Mental Health, whose participating sites in Europe, America, Asia and Australia pool genotyped patients with bipolar disorder treated with lithium, their long-term response rated retrospectively on the Alda scale, for genome-wide studies of the genetic basis of lithium response.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:20453537") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26806518") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:37433967") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:37433967") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11041653/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "ConLi+Gen is a global collaboration of scientists established to study the pharmacogenomics of lithium treatment in patients with BD [10].") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001619> "An international consortium of lithium researchers, established in 2008 on the initiative of the International Group for the Study of Lithium-Treated Patients (IGSLI) and the Unit on the Genetic Basis of Mood and Anxiety Disorders of the US National Institute of Mental Health, whose participating sites in Europe, America, Asia and Australia pool genotyped patients with bipolar disorder treated with lithium, their long-term response rated retrospectively on the Alda scale, for genome-wide studies of the genetic basis of lithium response.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001620> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001620>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2409050/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "All surveys used about 50 core CONOR questions agreed upon before the first CONOR survey in Tromsø in 1994.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001620> "A Norwegian multipurpose collection of health data and blood samples pooled from ten regional population-based health surveys conducted in 1994–2003, among them the fourth and fifth surveys of the Tromsø Study, the second Nord-Trøndelag Health Study (HUNT II), the Hordaland Health Study, the Oslo Health Study, the Oppland and Hedmark Health Study and the Troms and Finnmark Health Study. Participants, invited through the Population Registry of Norway, answered about 50 common core questions, had standardised examinations and gave non-fasting blood samples, and consented to linkage with national health registries; the collaboration is run by the Norwegian Institute of Public Health with the universities of Bergen, Oslo, Tromsø and Trondheim.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2409050/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Altogether 309 742 individuals were invited to the 10 surveys based on the 11-digit personal identifier and addresses from the Population Registry of Norway.4") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001620> "A Norwegian multipurpose collection of health data and blood samples pooled from ten regional population-based health surveys conducted in 1994–2003, among them the fourth and fifth surveys of the Tromsø Study, the second Nord-Trøndelag Health Study (HUNT II), the Hordaland Health Study, the Oslo Health Study, the Oppland and Hedmark Health Study and the Troms and Finnmark Health Study. Participants, invited through the Population Registry of Norway, answered about 50 common core questions, had standardised examinations and gave non-fasting blood samples, and consented to linkage with national health registries; the collaboration is run by the Norwegian Institute of Public Health with the universities of Bergen, Oslo, Tromsø and Trondheim.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2409050/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "At the screening, this initial questionnaire was collected from the attendees, participants underwent a physical examination and a non-fasting blood sample was drawn.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001620> "A Norwegian multipurpose collection of health data and blood samples pooled from ten regional population-based health surveys conducted in 1994–2003, among them the fourth and fifth surveys of the Tromsø Study, the second Nord-Trøndelag Health Study (HUNT II), the Hordaland Health Study, the Oslo Health Study, the Oppland and Hedmark Health Study and the Troms and Finnmark Health Study. Participants, invited through the Population Registry of Norway, answered about 50 common core questions, had standardised examinations and gave non-fasting blood samples, and consented to linkage with national health registries; the collaboration is run by the Norwegian Institute of Public Health with the universities of Bergen, Oslo, Tromsø and Trondheim.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2409050/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "During the years 1994–2003, a number of health surveys that were carried out in other counties and cities also provided similar data for the network. So far, 10 different surveys have provided data and blood samples for CONOR (Figure 1).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001620> "A Norwegian multipurpose collection of health data and blood samples pooled from ten regional population-based health surveys conducted in 1994–2003, among them the fourth and fifth surveys of the Tromsø Study, the second Nord-Trøndelag Health Study (HUNT II), the Hordaland Health Study, the Oslo Health Study, the Oppland and Hedmark Health Study and the Troms and Finnmark Health Study. Participants, invited through the Population Registry of Norway, answered about 50 common core questions, had standardised examinations and gave non-fasting blood samples, and consented to linkage with national health registries; the collaboration is run by the Norwegian Institute of Public Health with the universities of Bergen, Oslo, Tromsø and Trondheim.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2409050/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The CONOR collaboration is currently a research collaboration between the NIPH and the Universities of Bergen, Oslo, Tromsø and Trondheim.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001620> "A Norwegian multipurpose collection of health data and blood samples pooled from ten regional population-based health surveys conducted in 1994–2003, among them the fourth and fifth surveys of the Tromsø Study, the second Nord-Trøndelag Health Study (HUNT II), the Hordaland Health Study, the Oslo Health Study, the Oppland and Hedmark Health Study and the Troms and Finnmark Health Study. Participants, invited through the Population Registry of Norway, answered about 50 common core questions, had standardised examinations and gave non-fasting blood samples, and consented to linkage with national health registries; the collaboration is run by the Norwegian Institute of Public Health with the universities of Bergen, Oslo, Tromsø and Trondheim.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:17984119") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2409050/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The numbers in Table 1 include individuals who participated and had given their written consent for research and linkage to health registries.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001620> "A Norwegian multipurpose collection of health data and blood samples pooled from ten regional population-based health surveys conducted in 1994–2003, among them the fourth and fifth surveys of the Tromsø Study, the second Nord-Trøndelag Health Study (HUNT II), the Hordaland Health Study, the Oslo Health Study, the Oppland and Hedmark Health Study and the Troms and Finnmark Health Study. Participants, invited through the Population Registry of Norway, answered about 50 common core questions, had standardised examinations and gave non-fasting blood samples, and consented to linkage with national health registries; the collaboration is run by the Norwegian Institute of Public Health with the universities of Bergen, Oslo, Tromsø and Trondheim.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001621> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001621>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:28346442") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:28448241") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:32665703") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:33597508") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:34320204") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:33597508") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7890067/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "CIMBA DACC approval is required to access data from studies BCFR-ON, BRICOH, CONSIT TEAM, DKFZ, EMBRACE, FPGMX, G-FAST, GC-HBOC, GEMO, HEBCS, HEBON, IHCC, INHERIT, IOVHBOCS, MCGILL, NRG_ONCOLOGY, OUH and UKGRFOCR (see Supplementary Table 1—for a list of all CIMBA studies).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001621> "An Italian consortium of cancer genetics services and research groups studying hereditary breast cancer, among them the Fondazione IRCCS Istituto Nazionale dei Tumori and the Istituto Europeo di Oncologia in Milan and centres in Aviano, Bari, Florence, Genoa, Rome and Varese, with genetic testing by the Cogentech Cancer Genetic Test Laboratory in Milan. It contributes carriers of BRCA1 and BRCA2 pathogenic variants, most of them ascertained through cancer genetics clinics, as one study to the Consortium of Investigators of Modifiers of BRCA1 and BRCA2 (CIMBA) for research on genetic modifiers of cancer risk in carriers.")
