AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21775348") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38654257") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:41835011") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:41835011") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12988660/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The DNPR, PCRR, and the Danish National Health Service Register were linked to the cohorts and provided outcome data (see stable 1).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001666> "A national administrative register of the services provided under Denmark's public health insurance by the contractors of primary health care (general practitioners, practising specialists, dentists, physiotherapists, psychologists and others), which covers everyone living in Denmark with data from 1990 and records the citizen, the provider and the service but little clinical information; collected for administrative and research purposes, it is linked by personal identification number to the other national registers.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21775348") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:38654257") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:41835011") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:41835011") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12988660/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "To retrieve information from primary health care use (ie, general practitioners, physiotherapists, dentists, medical specialists in medical practices and psychologists) we used the Danish National Health Service Register.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001666> "A national administrative register of the services provided under Denmark's public health insurance by the contractors of primary health care (general practitioners, practising specialists, dentists, physiotherapists, psychologists and others), which covers everyone living in Denmark with data from 1990 and records the citizen, the provider and the service but little clinical information; collected for administrative and research purposes, it is linked by personal identification number to the other national registers.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27010749") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00691509") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A23664318+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "3159 women with grades I-III breast cancer were recruited 9-15 months post-diagnosis from 56 UK hospitals.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667> "A multicentre prospective cohort of women under 75 with grade I–III invasive primary breast cancer, recruited 9–15 months after diagnosis from 56 hospitals in the United Kingdom, who gave clinical, dietary, lifestyle, complementary-therapy and quality-of-life data and blood and urine samples every year for five years. Run from the University of Westminster, London, and funded by the charity Against Breast Cancer, it studies how dietary phytoestrogens and other diet and lifestyle factors relate to breast cancer recurrence and survival.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27010749") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00691509") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A23664318+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Detailed information on clinico-pathological, diet, lifestyle and quality of life is collected annually up to 5 years.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667> "A multicentre prospective cohort of women under 75 with grade I–III invasive primary breast cancer, recruited 9–15 months after diagnosis from 56 hospitals in the United Kingdom, who gave clinical, dietary, lifestyle, complementary-therapy and quality-of-life data and blood and urine samples every year for five years. Run from the University of Westminster, London, and funded by the charity Against Breast Cancer, it studies how dietary phytoestrogens and other diet and lifestyle factors relate to breast cancer recurrence and survival.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27010749") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00691509") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A23664318+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "DietCompLyf is a multi-centre prospective study designed to investigate associations between phytoestrogens - naturally occurring plant compounds with oestrogenic properties - and other diet and lifestyle factors with breast cancer recurrence and survival.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667> "A multicentre prospective cohort of women under 75 with grade I–III invasive primary breast cancer, recruited 9–15 months after diagnosis from 56 hospitals in the United Kingdom, who gave clinical, dietary, lifestyle, complementary-therapy and quality-of-life data and blood and urine samples every year for five years. Run from the University of Westminster, London, and funded by the charity Against Breast Cancer, it studies how dietary phytoestrogens and other diet and lifestyle factors relate to breast cancer recurrence and survival.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27010749") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00691509") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:27010749") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.nature.com/articles/bjc201666") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The University of Westminster curates the DietCompLyf database created by and funded by Against Breast Cancer (Registered Charity No. 1121258).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667> "A multicentre prospective cohort of women under 75 with grade I–III invasive primary breast cancer, recruited 9–15 months after diagnosis from 56 hospitals in the United Kingdom, who gave clinical, dietary, lifestyle, complementary-therapy and quality-of-life data and blood and urine samples every year for five years. Run from the University of Westminster, London, and funded by the charity Against Breast Cancer, it studies how dietary phytoestrogens and other diet and lifestyle factors relate to breast cancer recurrence and survival.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27010749") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00691509") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Data collected annually included blood and urine samples, which will now form the DietCompLyf Biobank, and detailed data including 7 day food diaries, food frequency questionnaires (FFQ), lifestyle and general health questionnaires.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667> "A multicentre prospective cohort of women under 75 with grade I–III invasive primary breast cancer, recruited 9–15 months after diagnosis from 56 hospitals in the United Kingdom, who gave clinical, dietary, lifestyle, complementary-therapy and quality-of-life data and blood and urine samples every year for five years. Run from the University of Westminster, London, and funded by the charity Against Breast Cancer, it studies how dietary phytoestrogens and other diet and lifestyle factors relate to breast cancer recurrence and survival.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27010749") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00691509") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Each patient participated from one year post-diagnosis of primary breast cancer and was on active follow-up for 5 years.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667> "A multicentre prospective cohort of women under 75 with grade I–III invasive primary breast cancer, recruited 9–15 months after diagnosis from 56 hospitals in the United Kingdom, who gave clinical, dietary, lifestyle, complementary-therapy and quality-of-life data and blood and urine samples every year for five years. Run from the University of Westminster, London, and funded by the charity Against Breast Cancer, it studies how dietary phytoestrogens and other diet and lifestyle factors relate to breast cancer recurrence and survival.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27010749") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00691509") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The study aimed to primarily explore the effect of dietary phytoestrogen consumption, but the effects of other diet and lifestyle practices and the use of complementary treatments were also investigated to identify differences between UK patients who develop secondary spread and those who do not.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667> "A multicentre prospective cohort of women under 75 with grade I–III invasive primary breast cancer, recruited 9–15 months after diagnosis from 56 hospitals in the United Kingdom, who gave clinical, dietary, lifestyle, complementary-therapy and quality-of-life data and blood and urine samples every year for five years. Run from the University of Westminster, London, and funded by the charity Against Breast Cancer, it studies how dietary phytoestrogens and other diet and lifestyle factors relate to breast cancer recurrence and survival.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:23664318") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:27010749") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00691509") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://www.againstbreastcancer.org.uk/our-research/prevention/dietcomplyf-study-diet-and-lifestyle") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4754625/supplementaryFiles") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Invasive primary breast cancer grade I-III, patients recruited 9 - 15 months after diagnosis, <age 75.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001667> "A multicentre prospective cohort of women under 75 with grade I–III invasive primary breast cancer, recruited 9–15 months after diagnosis from 56 hospitals in the United Kingdom, who gave clinical, dietary, lifestyle, complementary-therapy and quality-of-life data and blood and urine samples every year for five years. Run from the University of Westminster, London, and funded by the charity Against Breast Cancer, it studies how dietary phytoestrogens and other diet and lifestyle factors relate to breast cancer recurrence and survival.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001668> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001668>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00345930") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3637941/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Both children and adults are eligible to participate, the minimum subject age being 2 years.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001668> "The prospective registry study of the US Drug-Induced Liver Injury Network (DILIN), which the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases set up in 2003. Since September 2004 it has enrolled children aged 2 or over and adults referred to the network's clinical sites within 6 months of a suspected liver injury caused by a prescription or over-the-counter drug or a herbal or dietary supplement. Causality is adjudicated by expert review, clinical data and blood (DNA), serum and some tissue samples are collected, and confirmed cases are followed for at least 6 months, in order to study the clinical, genetic, environmental and immunological risk factors and the outcomes of drug-induced liver injury.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00345930") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3637941/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Causality is assessed with the widely used Roussel Uclaf Causality Assessment Method (RUCAM) as well as by ‘expert consensus’.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001668> "The prospective registry study of the US Drug-Induced Liver Injury Network (DILIN), which the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases set up in 2003. Since September 2004 it has enrolled children aged 2 or over and adults referred to the network's clinical sites within 6 months of a suspected liver injury caused by a prescription or over-the-counter drug or a herbal or dietary supplement. Causality is adjudicated by expert review, clinical data and blood (DNA), serum and some tissue samples are collected, and confirmed cases are followed for at least 6 months, in order to study the clinical, genetic, environmental and immunological risk factors and the outcomes of drug-induced liver injury.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00345930") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3637941/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Five clinical sites, a data coordinating centre and senior scientists from the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases initiated the DILIN prospective study in September 2004.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001668> "The prospective registry study of the US Drug-Induced Liver Injury Network (DILIN), which the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases set up in 2003. Since September 2004 it has enrolled children aged 2 or over and adults referred to the network's clinical sites within 6 months of a suspected liver injury caused by a prescription or over-the-counter drug or a herbal or dietary supplement. Causality is adjudicated by expert review, clinical data and blood (DNA), serum and some tissue samples are collected, and confirmed cases are followed for at least 6 months, in order to study the clinical, genetic, environmental and immunological risk factors and the outcomes of drug-induced liver injury.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00345930") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3637941/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Plasma, serum and lymphocytes for DNA extraction, EBV immortalization and proliferation assays are obtained and stored at the NIDDK biosample repository (Rutgers University, Piscataway, NJ, USA).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001668> "The prospective registry study of the US Drug-Induced Liver Injury Network (DILIN), which the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases set up in 2003. Since September 2004 it has enrolled children aged 2 or over and adults referred to the network's clinical sites within 6 months of a suspected liver injury caused by a prescription or over-the-counter drug or a herbal or dietary supplement. Causality is adjudicated by expert review, clinical data and blood (DNA), serum and some tissue samples are collected, and confirmed cases are followed for at least 6 months, in order to study the clinical, genetic, environmental and immunological risk factors and the outcomes of drug-induced liver injury.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00345930") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3637941/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The DILIN study aims to do this by creating within 6 months of onset a registry and tissue bank from people who have experienced liver injury due to prescription and over-the-counter drugs as well as herbal products.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001668> "The prospective registry study of the US Drug-Induced Liver Injury Network (DILIN), which the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases set up in 2003. Since September 2004 it has enrolled children aged 2 or over and adults referred to the network's clinical sites within 6 months of a suspected liver injury caused by a prescription or over-the-counter drug or a herbal or dietary supplement. Causality is adjudicated by expert review, clinical data and blood (DNA), serum and some tissue samples are collected, and confirmed cases are followed for at least 6 months, in order to study the clinical, genetic, environmental and immunological risk factors and the outcomes of drug-induced liver injury.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT00345930") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:19132805") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3637941/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK) established the Drug-Induced Liver Injury Network (DILIN) in 2003 to advance understanding and research into DILI by selecting five clinical sites and a data coordinating centre (DCC) that had submitted competitive grant applications.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001668> "The prospective registry study of the US Drug-Induced Liver Injury Network (DILIN), which the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases set up in 2003. Since September 2004 it has enrolled children aged 2 or over and adults referred to the network's clinical sites within 6 months of a suspected liver injury caused by a prescription or over-the-counter drug or a herbal or dietary supplement. Causality is adjudicated by expert review, clinical data and blood (DNA), serum and some tissue samples are collected, and confirmed cases are followed for at least 6 months, in order to study the clinical, genetic, environmental and immunological risk factors and the outcomes of drug-induced liver injury.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001670> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001670>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:24688116") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26446717") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:30060062") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:24688116") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4103672/fullTextXML") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Some cohorts only provided controls (GOYA, NTR, SU.VI.MAX, TwinsUK and WTCCC2) or only cases (BELFAST, CEPH centenarian cohort, Danish longevity study I/II, Leiden 85-plus Study I/II and Newcastle 85+ Study), whereas others contained both (Calabria cohort, deCODE, EGCUT, GEHA Study, German longevity study, Leiden Longevity Study, Rotterdam Study I/II and TwinGene).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001670> "A genetic study population of long-lived Danes, nonagenarians and centenarians drawn from the Longitudinal Study of Danish Centenarians, the Danish 1905 and 1911–12 Birth Cohort Studies, the Study of Danish Old Sibs and the Longitudinal Study of Ageing Danish Twins, used as the long-lived cases of studies of the genetics of human longevity.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:24688116") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26446717") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:30060062") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26446717") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4717275/") Annotation(rdfs:comment "The discovery study population, also referred to as the Danish Longevity Study (DKLS), consisted of participants drawn from the Longitudinal Study of Danish Centenarians (LSDC), the 1905 Birth Cohort Study, the 1911–12 Birth Cohort Study, the Study of Danish Old Sibs (DOS), and the Longitudinal Study of Ageing Danish Twins (LSADT).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001670> "A genetic study population of long-lived Danes, nonagenarians and centenarians drawn from the Longitudinal Study of Danish Centenarians, the Danish 1905 and 1911–12 Birth Cohort Studies, the Study of Danish Old Sibs and the Longitudinal Study of Ageing Danish Twins, used as the long-lived cases of studies of the genetics of human longevity.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:24688116") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:26446717") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:30060062") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26446717") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A26446717+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core") Annotation(rdfs:comment "we analyzed genomewide CNV data in 603 Danish nonagenarians and centenarians (mean age 96.9 years, range 90.0-102.5 years).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001670> "A genetic study population of long-lived Danes, nonagenarians and centenarians drawn from the Longitudinal Study of Danish Centenarians, the Danish 1905 and 1911–12 Birth Cohort Studies, the Study of Danish Old Sibs and the Longitudinal Study of Ageing Danish Twins, used as the long-lived cases of studies of the genetics of human longevity.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001671> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001671>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29531354") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:29531354") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41588-018-0058-3/MediaObjects/41588_2018_58_MOESM1_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "A total of 1,029 Caucasian patients with lacunar stroke, aged ≤70 years, were recruited from 72 specialist’s stroke centres throughout the UK between 2002 and 2012, as part of the Young Lacunar Stroke DNA Resource.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001671> "A UK case-control collection for genetic studies of lacunar stroke, in which Caucasian patients aged 70 or under with a clinical lacunar syndrome and a compatible subcortical infarct on MRI were recruited from 72 specialist stroke centres throughout the UK between 2002 and 2012, and unrelated Caucasian controls free of cerebrovascular disease were sampled, stratified by age and sex, from general practice lists in the same areas.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29531354") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:29531354") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41588-018-0058-3/MediaObjects/41588_2018_58_MOESM1_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Lacunar stroke was defined as a clinical lacunar syndrome, with an anatomically compatible lesion on MRI (subcortical infarct ≤15 mm in diameter).") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001671> "A UK case-control collection for genetic studies of lacunar stroke, in which Caucasian patients aged 70 or under with a clinical lacunar syndrome and a compatible subcortical infarct on MRI were recruited from 72 specialist stroke centres throughout the UK between 2002 and 2012, and unrelated Caucasian controls free of cerebrovascular disease were sampled, stratified by age and sex, from general practice lists in the same areas.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:29531354") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:29531354") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41588-018-0058-3/MediaObjects/41588_2018_58_MOESM1_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Unrelated Caucasian controls, free of clinical cerebrovascular disease, were obtained by random sampling, stratified for age and sex, from general practice lists from the same geographical location as the patients.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001671> "A UK case-control collection for genetic studies of lacunar stroke, in which Caucasian patients aged 70 or under with a clinical lacunar syndrome and a compatible subcortical infarct on MRI were recruited from 72 specialist stroke centres throughout the UK between 2002 and 2012, and unrelated Caucasian controls free of cerebrovascular disease were sampled, stratified by age and sex, from general practice lists in the same areas.")

# Individual: <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001673> (<http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001673>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21131975") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng.723/MediaObjects/41588_2011_BFng723_MOESM3_ESM.pdf") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21131975") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng.723/MediaObjects/41588_2011_BFng723_MOESM3_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Dutch controls were originally recruited within a population based survey (the Nijmegen Biomedical Study)") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001673> "A case–control study of renal cell carcinoma in the region of Nijmegen, the Netherlands, run by Radboud University Nijmegen Medical Centre with the Comprehensive Cancer Centre East, whose cases of European descent form a hospital-based series (patients of the hospital's outpatient urology clinic who had given blood and lifestyle data from 1999 onwards for studies of gene–environment interactions in urological disease and were found through the cancer registry to have renal cell carcinoma) and a population-based series recruited in 2009 through the regional cancer registry (patients diagnosed in 1995–2008 before the age of 75). Its controls are participants of the population-based Nijmegen Biomedical Study.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21131975") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng.723/MediaObjects/41588_2011_BFng723_MOESM3_ESM.pdf") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21131975") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng.723/MediaObjects/41588_2011_BFng723_MOESM3_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "From January 1999 onwards, 10 ml EDTA blood samples and lifestyle data have been collected from patients visiting the outpatient clinic for studies into gene-environment interactions for urological diseases.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001673> "A case–control study of renal cell carcinoma in the region of Nijmegen, the Netherlands, run by Radboud University Nijmegen Medical Centre with the Comprehensive Cancer Centre East, whose cases of European descent form a hospital-based series (patients of the hospital's outpatient urology clinic who had given blood and lifestyle data from 1999 onwards for studies of gene–environment interactions in urological disease and were found through the cancer registry to have renal cell carcinoma) and a population-based series recruited in 2009 through the regional cancer registry (patients diagnosed in 1995–2008 before the age of 75). Its controls are participants of the population-based Nijmegen Biomedical Study.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "PMID:21131975") Annotation(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng.723/MediaObjects/41588_2011_BFng723_MOESM3_ESM.pdf") Annotation(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:21131975") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng.723/MediaObjects/41588_2011_BFng723_MOESM3_ESM.pdf") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Patients diagnosed between 1995 and 2008 under the age of 75 years are selected and their vital status and current addresses updated through the hospital information systems of the 7 community hospitals and one university hospital (Radboud University Nijmegen Medical Centre, RUNMC) that are covered by the cancer registry.") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001673> "A case–control study of renal cell carcinoma in the region of Nijmegen, the Netherlands, run by Radboud University Nijmegen Medical Centre with the Comprehensive Cancer Centre East, whose cases of European descent form a hospital-based series (patients of the hospital's outpatient urology clinic who had given blood and lifestyle data from 1999 onwards for studies of gene–environment interactions in urological disease and were found through the cancer registry to have renal cell carcinoma) and a population-based series recruited in 2009 through the regional cancer registry (patients diagnosed in 1995–2008 before the age of 75). Its controls are participants of the population-based Nijmegen Biomedical Study.")
